{
  "concept": "A conic whose axes are tilted relative to the x,y axes needs an xy cross term in its equation; rotating the coordinate frame by the tilt angle theta aligns the new x',y' axes with the conic's own axes and makes the cross term vanish.",
  "figureType": "diagram",
  "elements": [
    "Thin black double-headed horizontal axis with arrowheads at both ends, labeled 'x' at its right end",
    "Thin black double-headed vertical axis with arrowheads at both ends, labeled 'y' at its top",
    "Origin where both frames meet (shared center, small dot)",
    "Orange double-headed rotated axis running lower-left to upper-right, labeled x' in orange italic at its upper-right arrowhead",
    "Orange double-headed rotated axis running lower-right to upper-left, labeled y' in orange italic at its upper-left arrowhead",
    "Dark navy-blue ellipse outline centered at the origin, major axis along x', minor axis along y', crossing both black axes",
    "Small black curved arc between the positive x-axis and the positive x'-axis with label 'θ'",
    "Dashed faint construction lines for the major and minor semi-axes of the ellipse (shown only when a point is selected)",
    "Selected point marker on the ellipse (filled dot) with dashed projection segments to the black x and y axes",
    "Dashed projection segments from the same point to the orange x' and y' axes",
    "Two coordinate readout callouts: black '(x, y) = …' and orange \"(x', y') = …\"",
    "Equation panel showing the general form Ax² + Bxy + Cy² = 1 with the Bxy term emphasized, and the rotated form x'²/a² + y'²/b² = 1",
    "Angle readout text 'θ = …°'",
    "Legend/key: black = original frame, orange = rotated frame, navy = the conic",
    "No tick marks or numeric labels on either axis"
  ],
  "interactions": [
    {
      "id": "thetaRotation",
      "type": "slider",
      "teaches": "That the tilt of the conic and the rotation of the primed frame are the same angle: only when θ matches the ellipse's tilt do the orange axes lie along the ellipse's own axes and the xy term drop out.",
      "label": "Rotation angle θ",
      "range": [0, 90, 1],
      "default": 35
    },
    {
      "id": "equationFrame",
      "type": "toggle",
      "teaches": "That the same ellipse has a messy equation with a cross term in the black frame and a clean standard-form equation in the aligned orange frame.",
      "label": "Equation shown in",
      "options": ["x,y frame (has Bxy term)", "x',y' frame (no cross term)"],
      "default": "x,y frame (has Bxy term)"
    },
    {
      "id": "pointCompare",
      "type": "click",
      "teaches": "That a single fixed point of the ellipse has two different coordinate pairs — one measured against the black axes, one against the orange axes — related by the rotation formulas."
    }
  ],
  "demo_steps": [
    {
      "title": "A tilted ellipse in the standard frame",
      "narration": "With θ set to 0 the orange axes sit exactly on top of the black ones, but the ellipse is still drawn tilted, so its equation in x and y carries a nonzero Bxy term.",
      "state": { "thetaRotation": 0, "equationFrame": "x,y frame (has Bxy term)" }
    },
    {
      "title": "Turn the frame toward the ellipse",
      "narration": "Drag θ upward and watch the orange axes swing counterclockwise while the black axes stay put; the cross-term coefficient shrinks as x' approaches the ellipse's long axis.",
      "state": { "thetaRotation": 20, "equationFrame": "x,y frame (has Bxy term)" }
    },
    {
      "title": "The alignment angle",
      "narration": "At θ ≈ 35° the x'-axis lies along the major axis and the y'-axis along the minor axis; the ellipse now looks 'straight' in the rotated frame.",
      "state": { "thetaRotation": 35, "equationFrame": "x,y frame (has Bxy term)" }
    },
    {
      "title": "The cross term disappears",
      "narration": "Switch the equation panel to the primed frame: the same curve is just x'²/a² + y'²/b² = 1 — standard form, no xy term at all.",
      "state": { "thetaRotation": 35, "equationFrame": "x',y' frame (no cross term)" }
    },
    {
      "title": "One point, two coordinate pairs",
      "narration": "Click any point on the ellipse to drop projections onto both frames; the point never moves, but its (x, y) and (x', y') readouts differ, which is exactly what the rotation formulas convert between.",
      "state": { "thetaRotation": 35, "equationFrame": "x',y' frame (no cross term)" }
    },
    {
      "title": "Over-rotate and lose it again",
      "narration": "Push θ past the alignment angle and the cross term returns with the opposite sign — the simplification happens at one specific θ, not at any rotation.",
      "state": { "thetaRotation": 65, "equationFrame": "x,y frame (has Bxy term)" }
    }
  ],
  "keyInsight": "The xy term in a conic's equation is not a property of the curve — it is a symptom of measuring a tilted curve with untilted axes; rotate the axes by the curve's own tilt angle and the term vanishes.",
  "reconstructionNotes": "Centered square canvas with the origin at the middle. Thin black double-headed x and y axes span the full width and height, arrowheads on all four ends, italic serif 'x' at the right end and 'y' at the top; no ticks or numbers. Overlay a pair of orange (vermilion, ~#E8622A) double-headed axes of the same length rotated counterclockwise by θ about the origin, with italic orange 'x'' at the upper-right arrowhead and 'y'' at the upper-left arrowhead. The ellipse is a dark navy (~#1F3F66) stroked outline, no fill, centered at the origin, with a clearly elongated major axis (roughly 2:1.3 axis ratio) lying along the x'-axis; it should visibly cross the black horizontal axis on both sides and extend above and below the black vertical axis. A small black arc of modest radius sits between the positive x and positive x' rays, labeled θ just outside it. When a point is selected, draw a filled navy dot on the ellipse with thin dashed black drop lines to the black axes and thin dashed orange drop lines to the orange axes, plus small coordinate callouts in the matching colors. Place the equation panel in a corner, color-matching the active frame: the Bxy term highlighted in black/gray when the unrotated form is shown, the clean x'²/a² + y'²/b² = 1 highlighted in orange when the rotated form is shown. Show the numeric θ value near the slider and near the arc."
}

