      }
    }
  ],
  "keyInsight": "The xy term in a conic's equation is not a property of the curve — it is a symptom of measuring a tilted curve with untilted axes; rotate the axes by the curve's own tilt angle and the term vanishes.",
  "reconstructionNotes": "Centered square canvas with the origin at the middle. Thin black double-headed x and y axes span the full width and height, arrowheads on all four ends, italic serif 'x' at the right end and 'y' at the top; no ticks or numbers. Overlay a pair of orange (vermilion, ~#E8622A) double-headed axes of the same length rotated counterclockwise by θ about the origin, with italic orange 'x'' at the upper-right arrowhead and 'y'' at the upper-left arrowhead. The ellipse is a dark navy (~#1F3F66) stroked outline, no fill, centered at the origin, with a clearly elongated major axis (roughly 2:1.3 axis ratio) lying along the x'-axis; it should visibly cross the black horizontal axis on both sides and extend above and below the black vertical axis. A small black arc of modest radius sits between the positive x and positive x' rays, labeled θ just outside it. When a point is selected, draw a filled navy dot on the ellipse with thin dashed black drop lines to the black axes and thin dashed orange drop lines to the orange axes, plus small coordinate callouts in the matching colors. Place the equation panel in a corner, color-matching the active frame: the Bxy term highlighted in black/gray when the unrotated form is shown, the clean x'²/a² + y'²/b² = 1 highlighted in orange when the rotated form is shown. Show the numeric θ value near the slider and near the arc."
}

